From 8492937e6639caad195dc64972a847d3064c7651 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alessandro Fontana <alessandro.fontana@pm.me>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 17:40:21 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] soc: samsung: exynos-pmu: prepare the core for PSCI
 power-down on exynos7870

The EL3 firmware of the Exynos7870 implements the PSCI CPU_SUSPEND
power-down state as a WFI that waits for the PMU to cut the core's
power, but does not program the PMU: the vendor kernel clears the core's
local power configuration (CPU_CONFIGURATION[3:0]) in its own cpuidle
driver before calling into the firmware. With the generic PSCI cpuidle
driver and nothing else, the core never comes back: it stays in the
firmware's WFI loop, powered, and an interrupt only wakes it into the
next WFI.

Measured with a test module that suspends one core with interrupts
disabled and wakes it with an IPI after a delay, sampling the core's
PMU status from another CPU:

  without clearing the configuration: the core is lost (status on,
                                      no more timer interrupts)
  clearing it first: 600/600 suspends return 0, the status reads
                     "off" for the whole sleep, and on wake-up the
                     configuration is already back to 0xf

Clear the configuration from a CPU PM notifier on CPU_PM_ENTER and set
it back on CPU_PM_EXIT and CPU_PM_ENTER_FAILED, for the case where the
firmware returns early because an interrupt is pending: a core left
with a cleared configuration would lose its power on the next plain
WFI. CPU_OFF needs none of this, the firmware programs the PMU itself.

---
 drivers/soc/samsung/Makefile         |  1 +
 drivers/soc/samsung/exynos-pmu.c     |  5 +++
 drivers/soc/samsung/exynos-pmu.h     |  3 ++
 drivers/soc/samsung/exynos7870-pmu.c | 66 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 4 files changed, 75 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 drivers/soc/samsung/exynos7870-pmu.c

